.Dd October 5, 2026
.Dt JOBD 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm jobd
.Nd job execution and scheduling service
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -system
.Op Fl -foreground
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the service behind
.Xr job 1 .
It accepts requests on a Unix socket, keeps the records of Jobs, Queues and
Groups, decides which waiting Job starts, and starts one supervisor process
for each running Job.
It runs in the foreground, writes its log to standard error and never
detaches; a service manager such as
.Xr systemd 1
or runit starts and restarts it.
.Pp
.Nm
is the same program as
.Xr job 1 ,
installed under a second name.
Every Job runs as the Unix user that runs
.Nm .
The service never changes to another user.
.Bl -tag -width "--foreground"
.It Fl -system
Use the system locations in
.Sx FILES
instead of the per-user ones.
Setting
.Ev JOB_SYSTEM
to 1 has the same effect and also selects the system service for a client.
.It Fl -foreground
Accepted for service scripts.
The foreground is the only mode.
.El
.Sh USER AND SYSTEM SERVICE
A user service belongs to one login account.
It keeps its files below that account's home directory, and its socket is
reachable by that account only.
This is the default.
.Pp
A system service is started with
.Fl -system
by a service account created for it.
It keeps its files in the conventional system directories.
Other accounts reach it only when a socket group is configured, see
.Sx ACCESS ,
and when their environment sets
.Ev JOB_SYSTEM
to 1.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -column "Runtime socket" "$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/job/job.sock" "/var/cache/job"
.It Sy Purpose Ta Sy User service Ta Sy System service
.It Configuration Ta Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/job/config.toml Ta Pa /etc/job/config.toml
.It State Ta Pa $XDG_STATE_HOME/job Ta Pa /var/lib/job
.It Runtime socket Ta Pa $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/job/job.sock Ta Pa /run/job/job.sock
.It Cache Ta Pa $XDG_CACHE_HOME/job Ta Pa /var/cache/job
.El
.Pp
An unset, empty or relative XDG variable is ignored and its fallback applies:
.Pa ~/.config ,
.Pa ~/.local/state
and
.Pa ~/.cache .
When neither the variable nor
.Ev HOME
is usable, the command fails and names the variables to set; no file is ever
placed in
.Pa /tmp .
.Pp
The state directory holds records, retained output, the working directories of
remote Jobs, the terminal sockets of running Jobs, the audit journal in
.Pa audit/ ,
the lifecycle event journal in
.Pa events/
and the lock
.Pa daemon.lock .
It is created with mode 0700.
When a socket group is configured, terminal sockets are placed in
.Pa terminals/
below the runtime directory instead, with mode 0660 in a directory of mode
0750, both owned by the group.
Nothing is stored in the cache directory yet.
.Pp
The socket is placed by the first rule that applies:
.Bl -enum
.It
.Ev JOB_RUNTIME_DIR
is set: the socket is
.Pa job.sock
in that directory.
.It
.Ev JOB_STATE_DIR
is set: the socket is
.Pa daemon.sock
inside that state directory.
A state directory that was relocated on purpose never shares the socket of the
account's ordinary service.
.It
System service:
.Pa /run/job/job.sock .
.It
.Ev XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
is set:
.Pa $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/job/job.sock .
.It
Otherwise, for example under runit without a login session:
.Pa daemon.sock
inside the state directory.
.El
.Pp
A client applies the same rules, tries that socket first and then
.Pa daemon.sock
in its state directory.
The service removes a socket left in the state directory by an earlier run
when it binds elsewhere.
A client of a user service that has neither
.Ev XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
nor one of the overrides, for example over
.Xr ssh 1
without a session manager, also tries
.Pa /run/user/ Ns Ar uid Ns Pa /job/job.sock .
In every other case the service and its clients must agree on these
variables.
A socket path longer than 107 bytes cannot be bound.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width JOB_RUNTIME_DIR
.It Ev JOB_SYSTEM
The value 1 selects the system locations.
.It Ev JOB_CONFIG
Configuration file.
A missing file named this way is an error.
.It Ev JOB_STATE_DIR
State directory.
.It Ev JOB_RUNTIME_DIR
Directory of the service socket.
.It Ev JOB_CACHE_DIR
Cache directory.
.It Ev JOB_CGROUP_ROOT
The cgroup the service manages, see
.Sx CGROUP DELEGATION .
.It Ev HOME , XDG_CONFIG_HOME , XDG_STATE_HOME , XDG_RUNTIME_DIR , XDG_CACHE_HOME
Defaults of a user service.
.It Ev LC_ALL , LC_MESSAGES , LANG
Language of messages; German is selected by a value beginning with de.
.El
.Sh CGROUP DELEGATION
Limits are enforced through a cgroup v2 subtree that the service owns.
The service chooses its root by the first rule that applies and names the rule
in its startup line, in
.Cm job doctor
and in
.Cm job host Fl -json :
.Bl -enum
.It
.Ev JOB_CGROUP_ROOT ,
or
.Cm root
in the
.Cm [cgroup]
table of
.Xr job.conf 5 ,
names it.
.It
The cgroup the service was started in, when the service may write its
.Pa cgroup.procs
and
.Pa cgroup.subtree_control .
If that cgroup is named
.Pa daemon ,
its parent is taken.
This is what
.Cm Delegate=yes
of a systemd unit provides.
.It
.Pa /sys/fs/cgroup/exec ,
the fixed path of earlier versions, when the service runs in it.
.El
.Pp
A root must hold no process other than the service itself.
A named root that holds other processes stops the start with an error.
A discovered one is skipped, and the startup line says why.
Without a root the service still runs: it watches processes and reports
.Dq limits watched, not enforced .
.Pp
Inside its root the service moves itself to
.Pa daemon ,
keeps supervisors in
.Pa shims
and Jobs below
.Pa jobs ,
and enables the cpu, memory, pids and io controllers the parent offers.
It changes nothing outside the root and sets no kernel parameter.
Unprivileged user namespaces, needed for
.Fl -net Cm none
and for proxy and tunnel networks, depend on these kernel settings, where
they exist; changing them is the administrator's decision:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
/proc/sys/user/max_user_namespaces
/proc/sys/kernel/unprivileged_userns_clone
/proc/sys/kernel/apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns
.Ed
.Sh ACCESS
The socket is the access boundary.
Every client the service admits has the same control over every Job, Queue,
Group and terminal; labels such as the session name are descriptions, not
permissions.
.Pp
By default the runtime directory has mode 0700 and the socket mode 0600, and
the service also refuses any peer whose user differs from its own.
.Pp
With
.Cm group
set in the
.Cm [socket]
table of
.Xr job.conf 5 ,
the runtime directory gets mode 0750 and the socket mode 0660, both owned by
that group, and a peer is admitted when it is the service user or when its
primary or supplementary groups contain the group.
The service user must be a member of the group.
An unknown group stops the start.
A shared socket needs a runtime directory apart from the state directory.
Records that name an actor store the peer's user ID.
.Pp
The state directory stays private in every case.
A client that cannot read it asks the service instead:
.Cm job run ,
.Cm job logs
and
.Cm job log
receive the retained output of one pinned attempt over the control socket,
read-only and with follow, and
.Cm job attach
connects to the terminal socket in the runtime directory.
.Cm job audit
and
.Cm job events
read the state directory directly and stay with the service user.
.Sh OPERATING MODE
With a delegated cgroup the service enforces limits through it.
Without one it runs in monitoring mode: processes are watched, limits are not
enforced, and the start line says
.Dq monitoring mode .
.Cm required
in the
.Cm [cgroup]
table of
.Xr job.conf 5
decides whether that is allowed.
With
.Cm required = true
the service refuses to start without a usable delegated cgroup and names what
is missing; with false, the default, it starts in monitoring mode.
In monitoring mode with the ordinary profile a Job that asks for
.Fl -mem ,
.Fl -pids
or a cgroup control such as
.Fl -memory-max
or
.Fl -cpu-limit
is refused with a capability error before it is recorded as runnable; the
legacy profile keeps watching
.Fl -mem
and
.Fl -pids .
.Pp
Limits set around the service still apply to it and to every Job.
.Cm job doctor
and
.Cm job host
report, as
.Li surrounding_limits ,
the tightest
.Pa memory.max ,
.Pa memory.high ,
.Pa cpu.max
and
.Pa pids.max
among the service's own cgroup and its ancestors with the cgroup that sets
each, the effective
.Pa cpuset.cpus ,
and the service's own resource limits.
Limits that cannot be read are reported as not discoverable.
.Sh SIGNALS AND RESTART
.Dv SIGTERM ,
.Dv SIGINT
and
.Dv SIGKILL
end the service at once.
It holds no state that is not already on disk.
Supervisors are separate processes in their own sessions and keep running.
A service that starts again takes the lock, reads the records, finds the
supervisors that still run by their process handles and boot identity, and
collects the results of those that ended meanwhile.
A reboot ends every supervisor; such attempts are recorded as lost.
.Pp
A service manager decides what a stop does to the other processes.
The supplied systemd units set
.Cm KillMode=process ,
so a stop or restart ends the service only, and
.Cm DelegateSubgroup=daemon ,
so the restarted service is placed beside the surviving Jobs.
Stopping the unit therefore does not stop Jobs; cancel them first with
.Xr job 1 .
The runit example behaves the same way.
.Pp
There is no reload signal.
.Cm job config reload
rereads the configuration while no Job is active or waiting.
The service profile, the socket group and the cgroup root change only by a
restart.
.Sh LOGGING
The service writes one line per event to standard error.
Under systemd this goes to the journal; under runit to the service's log
script.
The output of Jobs is not part of this log: it is kept in the state
directory and read with
.Cm job logs .
How much of each Job's output is kept is set by its output quota, and how
much the service keeps in total by its output budget; both are described
under
.Cm output
in
.Xr job.conf 5 .
.Sh LINGERING
A systemd user service ends when its account's last session ends, and
.Pa $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
is removed with it.
To keep a user service and its Jobs across logouts an administrator runs
.Cm loginctl enable-linger Ar user
once.
.Cm job doctor
reports whether lingering is enabled.
.Sh UPGRADE AND MIGRATION
Install the new program, then restart the service.
A running service keeps starting supervisors from the image it was started
from until then.
.Cm job doctor
compares the version and protocol of the service with those of the command.
.Pp
The state directory carries a schema version.
A service refuses state of another version; convert it offline with
.Cm job state validate ,
.Cm backup
and
.Cm migrate ,
described in the migration guide installed in
.Pa share/doc/job .
A state directory of the earlier name
.Pa exec
beside the default one stops the service and its clients until one is chosen
with
.Ev JOB_STATE_DIR .
.Pp
Earlier versions always kept the socket as
.Pa daemon.sock
in the state directory.
Clients of this version still find it there.
Clients of an earlier version do not find a socket bound in a runtime
directory; replace them together with the service.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
runs until it is ended.
It exits with 125 when it cannot start, after naming the reason on standard
error.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Run a user service in a terminal:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
jobd
.Ed
.Pp
Enable the supplied units:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
systemctl --user enable --now jobd
systemctl enable --now jobd
.Ed
.Pp
Check a host before or after starting the service:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
job doctor
job doctor --system --json
job --system queue
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr job 1 ,
.Xr job.conf 5 ,
.Xr job 7 ,
.Xr cgroups 7 ,
.Xr systemd.resource-control 5 ,
.Xr loginctl 1
